Title:
OpenUDC2 — Open source implementation of Cobalt Strike’s UDC2 spec

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
OpenUDC2 is a proof-of-concept open source implementation of the UDC2 (User-Defined Communication 2) specification introduced in Cobalt Strike, intended to let non–Cobalt Strike C2 frameworks interoperate with existing UDC2 channel modules. It provides an Adaptix C2-based reference implementation consisting of a modified beacon agent that speaks the OpenUDC2 protocol and a listener that mimics the Cobalt Strike UDC2 listener behavior. The project’s goal is portability: it avoids Adaptix-specific dependencies so other C2s with custom agent/listener specs can adopt it. It enables reuse/testing of community UDC2 modules (e.g., ICMP transport) without requiring Cobalt Strike. The author notes the PoC does not add extra encryption and should not be used “live” without implementing strong cryptography. It’s primarily useful for red team tool developers and C2 framework authors, and secondarily for defenders wanting to understand/replicate UDC2 traffic patterns for detection.

Technical Focus:
- UDC2 protocol/spec interoperability (Cobalt Strike-compatible)
- Custom C2 transport/channel modules (e.g., ICMP UDC2)
- Agent + listener protocol design (beacon-side and server-side)
- BOF (Beacon Object File) integration and embedding
- Adaptix C2 extender/plugin architecture (reference implementation)

Use Cases:
- Port Cobalt Strike UDC2 channel modules to an open source C2 framework
- Build and test alternative C2 transports (ICMP, DNS, etc.) using the UDC2 model
- Develop a Cobalt Strike–compatible UDC2 listener for research/lab environments
- Create detection engineering test fixtures by reproducing UDC2-like traffic flows
- Prototype new UDC2 modules against a non-CS implementation

Keywords:
UDC2, Cobalt Strike, beacon, BOF, Beacon Object File, custom transport, C2 framework, Adaptix C2, listener, agent, extender, icmp-udc2, udc2-vs, protocol compatibility, command-and-control, ICMP channel, red team tooling, interoperability, encryption warning